6 types of Bean scopes in spring framework

1. singleton
When we define a bean with the singleton scope, the container creates a single instance of that bean;
all requests for that bean name will return the same object, which is cached.
Any modifications to the object will be reflected in all references to the bean.
This scope is the default value if no other scope is specified.

2. prototype
A bean with the prototype scope will return a different instance every time it is requested from the container.
It is defined by setting the value prototype to the @Scope annotation in the bean definition

3. request
4. session
5. application
6. websocket

among the 6 these ones are only available in a web-aware application which are: request, session, application and websocket

Design Patterns

1. Creational

a) Singleton
- Spring beans by default are singletons eg a class with @Service will be a singleton as it should only have one instance

b) Factory
- used in bean factory and app context. It is also used in creating objects dynamically based on configs or conditions
eg  @Bean
       public UserService userService() {
           return new UserService(); // Factory method creating an object
       }


c) Builder
- Creation of complex objects in a readable manner.
- Often used in DTOs mainly by use of the @Builder annotation using lombok but can be explicitly set without lombok
- usage is in the DTOs where we have a builder lombok

d) Abstract

e) Prototype
- used each time a new instance of a bean is required each time its injected
- use of the @Scope("prototype") on top of the bean is essential
- usage in projects not seen yet

2. Structural

a) Adapter
- used when integrating to an API which has different fields as our service
- used in Open market
- integrating to a payment gateway with XML responses and converting to json mapping fields

b) Bridge
- making use of an interface with the same method used in different platforms with different implementations.
- Multi class implementation support
- Open market on the success and failure callback methods

c) Composite
d) Decorator
- used in adding logging to business logging

e) Facade
- use of an interface to hide business logic of implementation when an API call is done from controller to business layer
- or calling an implementation form another class using an interface reference as a facade

f) Flyweight
g) Proxy
- used in AOP by logging service implementations via aspect programming
- not really seen yet in project


3. Behavioral

a) Chain of responsibility
- private methods to validate incoming requests for creation and updating mostly
- open market requests to check if requests are valid
- also in exception handling in scenarios of record not found or illegal state exceptions in OM

b) Command
- extending the JavaDelegate in Camunda delegates
- JavaDelegate follows this principle by encapsulating business logic inside a reusable class, which is then executed by the workflow engine.

c) Iterator
- Iterator Pattern concept is somewhat similar to how a Spring Data Repository works when retrieving user details from DB
in our case it could be claims and policies

d) Mediator
 - used in Rest Controller classes by use of the @RestController annotation
 between the service implementation and client Api calls

e) Memento
- example Shopping Cart with Undo Feature
- A user can add or remove products from a shopping cart, and if they make a mistake, they can undo the last action.
- in IWX no example found

f) Observer
- code base to send emails making use of the asynchronous feature
- usually after the event of a user account having been created and send the update

g) State
- not yet identified
- Order Processing System

h) Strategy
- used in open market to have separate implementations for failure and success callbacks from vopd
- use of different payment methods, once in Instapay

i) Template method
- RestTemplate, by providing a predefined algorithm for executing HTTP requests while allowing customization through callbacks.

How the Template Method Works in RestTemplate

    Defines a Standard Workflow
        Opens a connection
        Serializes request data
        Sends the request
        Handles response or errors
        Closes the connection
    Allows Customization via Callbacks
        RequestCallback for modifying the request
        ResponseExtractor for customizing response handling
        ErrorHandler for exception handling

j) Visitor
 - not yet identified, dont think we have used it in our project
 - Logging Different Entities in a Spring Boot Application
 - in IWX Camunda's Decision Model and Notation (DMN) engine, rules are evaluated using a Visitor-like pattern.
          Each decision table is traversed and evaluated row by row.